WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.320
You might think about kindness

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.240
as being this quite soft, fluffy,
wishy-washy thing,

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.320
but actually, it's really fundamental
to how we connect with each other.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.640
I think being kind is part
of the purpose of being alive.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.720
Acts of kindness are needed in the
world, right now more than ever.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:28.280
Back in 2011, I was sitting in
a café, just enjoying a breakfast,

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.560
when I looked up at a screen

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.160
and there was a double-decker bus
on fire in London.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.560
It was terrifying.
It looked like civil war.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.080
And there was a very negative
response to the riots

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.880
that really upset me as well.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.960
I'd felt increasingly despairing

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.360
about what felt like the enormity
of the problems of the world.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.960
I didn't know what I could do.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.160
And he was overwhelmed
with gratitude,

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.160
it was disproportionate to my tiny
amount of time and money.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.880
But I thought, I did kind of
put a smile on his face,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.600
that sort of did make a difference.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.280
So as I was going home,
I concocted this foolhardy notion

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.680
that I was going to try and do an
act of kindness every single day

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.280
for a stranger for a year.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.880
It was completely life-transforming.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.520
It's one of the big paradoxes
of kindness,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.640
that an act of kindness that
is intended to benefit others

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.480
actually had some positive
consequences for yourself.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.680
There are patterns of activation
in the brain

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.720
which correspond
to a boost to wellbeing.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.200
The reward pathways in the brain
are activated

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.320
when people are performing kind acts.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.880
Those relationships that are required
for working cooperatively

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.440
are founded upon basic
social connections.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.000
So it's pretty fundamental

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:25.800
to how human beings
interact with each other.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.360
Thank you so much,
that's really kind of you.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.200
Oh, yes. So over the course
of the year,

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.600
it proved itself to be
utterly heart-warming,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.880
completely terrifying,
occasionally expensive,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:43.400
sometimes physically hazardous,

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.960
like when I carried some
really heavy shopping

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.040
over four miles to a lady's flat.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.680
So it was a really,
sort of, creative journey,

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.600
that was also tiring
and incredibly inspiring.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.360
Kindness changes the world!

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.560
I was like high every day.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.160
Mostly it was kind of a warm glow

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.280
round your heart and also your tummy.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.120
It just felt really good.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.720
Human beings have a predisposition

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.640
to exhibit kindness to other people.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.400
But they also have the possibility

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.360
of demonstrating quite significant
unkindness to other people.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.920
The environment makes
a huge difference.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.520
All of those stories about
kindness being weak,

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.560
we have to challenge those now.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.240
When you think of a really
successful person

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.720
do you think of someone who's kind?

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.400
Or do you think of someone
who's out there in the limelight,

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.360
really dominant figure, a celebrity,
who's very wealthy?

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.360
What can we do to turn the narrative
of success around,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.480
so we say that actually
being successful

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.520
does involve positive relationships
with other people.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.880
This is one of my favourites.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.440
Some old people dancing.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.960
I just thought about
how elderly people

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.520
had no contact beyond
the walls of their care homes.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.480
What started off as quite
a small localised idea

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.880
just became something way bigger
than I could ever have imagined.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.720
It's reached over 250 schools
and over 250 care homes

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.720
and positively impacted
thousands of people.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.680
What we witnessed during that time

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.800
was how much we yearn
to help each other.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.800
What we have to do now is remember

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.320
the immense kindness that we were
capable of during this time.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.520
This can't simply be a matter
of instructing people

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.640
in a given setting to be kind.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.040
Hey, you. You need to be kind.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.680
We need to change our environment
so that it feels normative

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:04.680
to be kind.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.880
I would really like to see
businesses, schools, hospitals

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.440
all public services,
have a kindness manifesto.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.880
So that they all ask themselves,
"Is this kind?"

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.880
So that it becomes an ordinary
part of our conversation

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.640
at every level in every
organisation, everywhere.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.200
The biggest lesson for me
was to embrace the fact

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.840
that every single day
I can do something.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.560
That thing might be just saying
good morning to someone.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.400
That thing might be just smiling.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.080
That's how we change the world.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.640
If you carry out a good deed
to someone else

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.440
that will increase the chance of them

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.320
carrying out kind deeds
to other people.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.640
So it kind of goes
in a circle, kindness.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.520
The small stuff may actually
be the big stuff.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.480
All those small things that you
figure, "Well, they're not important,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.160
just people being nice
to each other."

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.640
Maybe that's the most important
thing for creating an environment

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.640
which actually enables
people to feel good

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.680
and to be able to work together

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.680
and to be able to take on
some really big challenges.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:13.640
In the same way that a beach is
filled with a billion grains of sand,

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.480
there's this sort
of beautiful humility 

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.640
in saying, "I can, at every single
moment, at any point of any day,

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.280
contribute to making the world
a better place."

